teacher_answer:
The height of the pile of three pipes is calculated by considering the vertical distance from the base to the top of the top pipe. The centers of the three pipes form an equilateral triangle with side length 10 cm. The height of this triangle is (sqrt(3)/2)*10 = 5*sqrt(3) cm. Adding the radius of the top pipe (5 cm) to this height gives the total height of the pile.

**Answer:** The height \( h \) of the pile is \( 5(\sqrt{3} + 1) \) cm. So, \( h = 5(\sqrt{3} + 1) \) cm.

\boxed{5(\sqrt{3} + 1)}

