teacher_answer:
To find the matrix that corresponds to rotating about the origin by an angle of \(45^\circ\) clockwise, we start with the general rotation matrix for counterclockwise rotations:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta & -\sin\theta \\
\sin\theta & \cos\theta
\end{bmatrix}
\]

For a clockwise rotation by \(45^\circ\), we use \(\theta = -45^\circ\). Substituting \(\theta = -45^\circ\) into the matrix, we get:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos(-45^\circ) & -\sin(-45^\circ) \\
\sin(-45^\circ) & \cos(-45^\circ)
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Using the properties of trigonometric functions, we know that \(\cos(-\theta) = \cos\theta\) and \(\sin(-\theta) = -\sin\theta\). Therefore, the matrix becomes:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos45^\circ & \sin45^\circ \\
-\sin45^\circ & \cos45^\circ
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Since \(\cos45^\circ = \sin45^\circ = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\), substituting these values into the matrix gives:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\
-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{bmatrix}
\]

Thus, the rotation matrix for a \(45^\circ\) clockwise rotation is:

\[
\boxed{\begin{bmatrix} \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \\ -\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} & \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \end{bmatrix}}
\]

